Series = {International Multidisciplinary Scientific Conferences on Social
   Sciences and Arts},
Year = {2014},
Pages = {351-358},
Note = {International Multidisciplinary Scientific Conferences on Social
   Sciences and Arts (SGEM 2014), Albena, BULGARIA, SEP 01-10, 2014},
Organization = {Bulgarian Acad Sci; Acad Sci Czech Republ; Latvian Acad Sci; Polish Acad
   Sci; Russian Acad Sci; Serbian Acad Sci \& Arts; Slovak Acad Sci Intro;
   Natl Acad Sci Ukraine; Natl Acad Sci Armenia; Sci Counc Japan; World
   Acad Sci TWAS; European Acad Sci, Arts \& Lett; Acad Fine Arts Zagreb
   Croatia; Croatian Acad Sci \& Arts; Acad Sci Moldova; Montenegrin Acad
   Sci \& Arts; Georgian Acad Sci; Acad Fine Arts \& Design Bratislava;
   Russian Acad Arts; Turkish Acad Sci; SGEM},
Abstract = {Motivation of addicted clients is perceived as a part of the ``essential
   treatment-related perceptions{''} complex. The level of motivation when
   entering treatment significantly influences the duration of treatment
   and its outcome. Therefore, motivation is very important and the
   knowledge of particular components essentially influences possible
   interventions and maintenance of abstinence of an addicted person. We
   also suppose that it can be a predictor of premature dropout. One of
   cited components is external motivation which is a base for addicted
   client's decision to enter treatment. Various life situations may be a
   motivational factor for seeking treatment. As for addictions, there are
   often problems with law, money, health, family, employment. These are
   individual and variable in time. A user perceives them as an external
   pressure which a treatment has to use in order to maintain a client in
   the process of change. The paper presents results of a research
   conducted on a research set of 138 clients before entering 15
   resocialization centres in the Slovak Republic in the course of one
   year. EuropASI and CMRS standardized questionnaires were used as
   research tools. The research shows the level of motivation and external
   circumstances which are crucial for entering and maintaining clients in
   a resocialization process. Among identified external circumstances,
   there is also high seriousness of problems in relationship problems in a
   family system and with people outside a family. This finding points to
   the legitimacy of social work and its methods and justifies the changes
   in a system of work with an addicted client towards a systemic work with
   the whole family.},
