   Healthy Human Being and Environment, Vilnius, LITHUANIA, MAY 22-27, 2012},
Organization = {IFEH; Minist Hlth Republ Lithuania; LUHE},
Abstract = {In France, specialists of environmental health are also required among
   the health professionals (i.e., doctor, pharmacist, nurse.). This point
   is highlighted in the last French National Program of Environmental
   Health (i.e., PNSE2). Moreover, this need is also expressed by the
   French population in opinions polls (Barometre Sante Environnement,
   2007): most of interrogated people mentioned their perception of
   environmental hazards on health and their fears to develop an
   environmental illness during life. The intervention of a health
   professional can be efficient to change individual behavior and to guide
   them towards the promotion of environmental health. Today, the formation
   in environmental health can be promoted in the context of French
   reforms, as well for the initial training of students in Pharmacy, as
   for the continuing education of pharmacist (in relation with the new
   missions proposed to the pharmacist by the French Hospital, Patient,
   Health and Health Territory Law (HPST Law)). The multidisciplinary
   expertise of the pharmacist (i.e., knowledge in toxicology, epidemiology
   (essential in the health risk assessment), chemistry (essential in
   metrology)) allows him to easily communicate with others health
   professionals and thus maintains him as a pertinent interlocutor for
   health advices, in direct contact with patients, without appointment
   unlike with medical doctor. However, some additional specific training
   on the environmental aspects is desirable. The actions initiated in
   initial training of the pharmacists, for continuing education (i.e.,
   Master's Degree of ``Risk Sciences in Health{''}) and the results of an
   opinion poll performed during a public health symposium at the
   University of Auvergne (France) can illustrate this point-of-view.},
