Note = {10th International Conference on Indoor Air Quality and Climate (Indoor
   Air 2005), Beijing, PEOPLES R CHINA, SEP 04-09, 2005},
Organization = {Tsinghua Univ; Minist Educ China; Natl Nat Sci Fdn China; Beijing
   Municipal Commiss Sci \& Technol; Shanghai Municipal Commiss Sci \&
   Technol; US EPA; Amer Ind Hyg Assoc; Amer Soc Heating, Refrigerating \&
   Air Conditioning Engineers; Int Commiss Occupat Hlth, SC Indoor Air Qual
   \& Hlth; Int Council Res \& Innovat Bldg \& Construct; European Commiss
   Asia Pro Eco Programme Promot Sustainable Solut Environm Europe \& Asia;
   Korean Soc Indoor Environm; Soc Indoor Environm},
Abstract = {To investigate indoor air quality (IAQ) and the impact factors of IAQ in
   domestic buildings, we carried out a field study in 18 selected houses
   in Dublin and the surrounding area between September 2004 and February
   2005. Indoor temperature, relative humidity (RH), concentrations of
   selected pollutants including carbon dioxide (CO(2)) carbon monoxide
   (CO), particulate matters (PM(1), PM(2.5), PM(7) and PM(10)), moisture,
   and ozone (03) were continuously measured in each house. The
   infiltration rates in ten houses were tested with both fan
   pressurization and tracer gas decay methods. During the measurements, a
   questionnaire and a log sheet were filled in by the householder which
   provided details of building features, ambient air condition, occupancy,
   HVAC facilities, inhabitant life style, household energy consumption and
   their perceptions of the indoor climate. The results indicate that: 1.
   The average indoor temperature and RH varies quite a lot in the
   particular sampled houses/rooms. This causes unique air circulation in
   every single house combined with other factors like construction,
   openings etc. Thermal conditions, on the other hand are well controlled
   by the occupants according to their self-reports. 2 The time variations
   of contaminant concentrations showed the influence from pollutant
   sources, occupant activities and infiltration/ventilation
   characteristics. 3. Episodic peaks are easily found in the pollutants of
   most houses, but some houses with very high levels of short-term
   exposures to CO, CO, or Ozone need more careful assessment. 4. Some
   correlations between the measured parameters are obvious, while some
   uncertainties have to be further investigated.},
